::CW_U03_L05_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The narrator describes a cracked cup each time the family avoids talking about moving. What is the writer's likely purpose?{
~A. To explain how cups are made.#Incorrect. The passage is not informational. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. To make the cup a visible sign of family tension.#Correct. The repeated cup connects object detail to emotional conflict.
~C. To remove tension from the scene.#Incorrect. The repeated image builds tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. To show that moving is never discussed anywhere.#Incorrect. It is discussed indirectly through avoidance. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: "Naturally, the committee chose the plan that solved nothing and cost twice as much." What rhetorical choice is strongest?{
~A. Neutral description.#Incorrect. The wording has judgment. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Celebration.#Incorrect. The sentence criticizes the plan. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Scientific definition.#Incorrect. It is not defining a term. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Sarcasm.#Correct. "Naturally" creates a critical, ironic attitude. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q03::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The writer delays revealing why the narrator will not enter the house until the last paragraph. What is the likely reader effect?{
=A. It creates suspense and curiosity.#Correct. Delayed explanation makes readers wonder. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. It proves houses are unimportant.#Incorrect. The house matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It removes structure.#Incorrect. Delay is a structure choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It makes the ending impossible to understand.#Incorrect. It can clarify later. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: "The trophy glittered under the lights, but Mira kept looking at the empty seat in the front row." What craft choice supports purpose?{
~A. A recipe list.#Incorrect. No recipe appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Random weather detail.#Incorrect. No weather appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Contrast between public success and private absence.#Correct. The trophy and empty seat create emotional contrast.
~D. Removing all emotion.#Incorrect. The contrast creates emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: A writer wants readers to distrust a narrator. Which choice best supports that purpose?{
~A. The narrator uses paragraphs.#Incorrect. Paragraphs do not create distrust by themselves.
~B. The narrator names a color once.#Incorrect. That is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The narrator describes lunch accurately.#Incorrect. Accuracy does not create distrust. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The narrator gives three different versions of the same event.#Correct. Contradiction creates doubt. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The writer uses a gentle tone when describing a rival's mistake. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To make the rival seem monstrous.#Incorrect. Gentle tone does the opposite. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. To invite sympathy instead of ridicule.#Correct. Gentle tone guides a compassionate reader response.
~C. To remove reader response.#Incorrect. Tone shapes response. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. To explain grammar only.#Incorrect. This is a craft effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: "The hallway narrowed with every step toward the principal's office." What reader effect does the image create?{
=A. It makes anxiety feel physical.#Correct. The hallway image reflects pressure. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. It provides exact architecture measurements.#Incorrect. It is figurative perception. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It creates a happy celebration.#Incorrect. The image feels tense. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It removes the setting.#Incorrect. The hallway is setting. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: A writer repeats the phrase "not yet" as a character postpones telling the truth. What is the purpose of repetition?{
~A. To make the scene unrelated.#Incorrect. It connects to character action. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. To prove the character already confessed.#Incorrect. The character postpones confession. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. To emphasize delay and avoidance.#Correct. Repetition mirrors repeated postponement. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. To avoid rhetoric.#Incorrect. Repetition is rhetorical. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The scene opens with the final argument, then moves backward to show the small choices that caused it. What structural purpose is likely?{
~A. To remove cause and effect.#Incorrect. The flashback shows causes. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. To make time irrelevant.#Incorrect. Time structure matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. To list unrelated events.#Incorrect. The events explain the conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. To make readers ask how the conflict reached that point.#Correct. Starting at the argument creates a question.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q10::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: "She smiled so widely that her brother stepped back." What does the detail likely do?{
=A. It hints that the smile may hide anger or pressure.#Correct. The brother's reaction complicates the smile.
~B. It proves the scene has no tension.#Incorrect. Stepping back suggests tension. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It explains dentistry.#Incorrect. No dental information appears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It removes character response.#Incorrect. The brother responds. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: A writer wants to show grief without naming it. Which craft choice best fits?{
~A. Say "She was sad" five times.#Incorrect. That names emotion directly and bluntly. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Show the character setting two plates at dinner, then putting one back.#Correct. The action implies absence and grief. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Add unrelated sports scores.#Incorrect. That distracts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove all action.#Incorrect. Action can show emotion. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The narrator uses formal, distant language while describing a painful memory. What reader effect might this create?{
~A. It always means the memory is funny.#Incorrect. Formal distance does not guarantee humor. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It removes voice.#Incorrect. Formal distance is a voice choice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It proves the narrator is not affected.#Incorrect. It may show avoidance. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. It can show emotional restraint or avoidance.#Correct. Distance in language can reveal controlled pain.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: The writer gives the antagonist one quiet moment helping a child. What purpose might this serve?{
~A. To prove the character has no role.#Incorrect. It deepens the role. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. To remove all conflict.#Incorrect. Complexity can increase conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. To complicate the reader's view of the character.#Correct. A humane moment adds complexity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. To switch genres automatically.#Incorrect. One moment does not determine genre. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L05_Q14::[MLA.CW.WR.02] Passage: "The clock clicked louder each time Ren avoided the question." What craft choice is used?{
